  <!-- Real operational note instead of generic setup tips -->
  <h2>Two Things eSIM Card Doesn't Spell Out on Its Own Site</h2>
  <p>
    The fair-use language on eSIM Card's unlimited plans reads: "Unlimited bundles provide an allowed limit of data at full speed per 24 hours and thereafter unlimited data at lower speeds." That's their own wording, and it confirms tethering/hotspot works on unlimited plans and that the plan activates on first use rather than at purchase. What it doesn't do is give you the actual number: how many GB at full speed before the slowdown kicks in. Some competitors publish that figure directly (<a href="/saily/">Saily</a>, for instance, states 5GB in a rolling 24 hours in most destinations, 3GB in some); eSIM Card just says "an allowed limit" without naming one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The refund policy is also stricter than it might look at a glance. eSIM Card's own policy page describes eSIMs as fundamentally non-refundable once installed or used, with refunds limited to narrow cases like a genuine coverage failure or a technical fault on their end where zero data was consumed, and even then a claim has to be filed within 30 days of purchase. Buy the plan you're confident about, not one you're hoping to test and return.
  </p>

